# C++ Notes
# MyString Class — Constructors, Copy Constructor & Rule of Three

> [!abstract]
> **Goal**
>
> This project is **not** intended to replace `std::string`.
>
> Its purpose is to understand how C++ manages memory internally by implementing a simplified string class from scratch.

---

# Why create our own String class?

Normally we would simply write

```cpp
std::string name{"Physics"};
```

and everything would work automatically.

However, `std::string` hides many important concepts from us.

By creating our own class we learn:

- Classes & Objects
- Constructors
- Destructor
- Dynamic Memory Allocation
- Heap Memory
- Deep Copy
- Shallow Copy
- Memory Ownership
- RAII
- Rule of Three

---

# Class Layout

```text
MyString
│
├── char *str
│
├── Constructors
├── Destructor
├── display()
├── get_length()
└── get_str()
```

Every object owns exactly **one dynamically allocated C-style string**.

---

# Memory Ownership

Our object owns its memory.

```text
MyString object

      str
       │
       ▼

+---+---+---+---+---+----+
| L | a | r | r | y | \0 |
+---+---+---+---+---+----+
```

Since **our class allocated this memory**, our class is also responsible for deleting it.

> [!important]
> **Golden Rule**
>
> Whoever allocates memory must eventually release it.

---

# Constructors

Constructors initialize newly created objects.

Whenever an object is created, the compiler automatically calls one constructor.

Example

```cpp
MyString first;
```

Compiler automatically executes

```cpp
MyString::MyString();
```

---

# No-Args Constructor

## Purpose

Creates an empty string.

Instead of storing

```cpp
str = nullptr;
```

we allocate

```cpp
new char[1];
```

and store

```cpp
'\0'
```

Memory

```text
str

 ▼

+----+
| \0 |
+----+
```

### Why?

Functions like

```cpp
strlen(str);
```

expect a valid C-string.

Calling

```cpp
strlen(nullptr);
```

results in **Undefined Behavior**.

Therefore every object should always remain in a valid state.

---

# Overloaded Constructor

Example

```cpp
MyString name{"Larry"};
```

### Step 1

Determine the string length.

```cpp
strlen(s);
```

returns

```
Larry

↓

5
```

---

### Step 2

Allocate memory.

```cpp
new char[strlen(s)+1];
```

The extra `+1` stores the null terminator (`'\0'`).

---

### Step 3

Copy every character.

```cpp
strcpy(str,s);
```

Memory becomes

```text
source

Larry

↓

this

Larry
```

Different memory

Same contents

---

# Copy Constructor

Example

```cpp
MyString first{"Physics"};

MyString second{first};
```

Compiler automatically calls

```cpp
MyString(const MyString &source);
```

---

# Why can't we simply copy the pointer?

Suppose we write

```cpp
str = source.str;
```

Memory

```text
source.str
      │
      ▼
 Physics

this->str
      │
      └───────────────┘
```

Now both objects point to exactly the same memory.

When one object dies

```cpp
delete[] str;
```

the memory disappears.

The second object still points to deleted memory.

Later its destructor executes

```cpp
delete[] str;
```

again.

Result

- Double Delete
- Dangling Pointer
- Undefined Behavior

This is called

> **Shallow Copy**

---

# Deep Copy

Instead we allocate completely new memory.

```cpp
str = new char[strlen(source.str)+1];

strcpy(str,source.str);
```

Memory

```text
Object A

Physics

Object B

Physics
```

Different addresses

Same contents

Each object owns its own memory.

---

# Destructor

Purpose

Release dynamically allocated memory.

```cpp
delete[] str;
```

Whenever an object leaves its scope

the compiler automatically executes

```cpp
~MyString();
```

Example

```cpp
{
    MyString s{"Physics"};

}
```

At the closing brace

```cpp
s.~MyString();
```

is called automatically.

---

# Getter Functions

Getter functions provide read-only access to private data.

Example

```cpp
int get_length() const;
```

The keyword

```cpp
const
```

means

> "This function promises not to modify the object."

---

# Why return const char* ?

Instead of

```cpp
char*
```

we return

```cpp
const char*
```

This prevents outside code from writing

```cpp
myString.get_str()[0]='X';
```

which would directly modify the object's internal memory.

This is called **Encapsulation**.

---

# Rule of Three

Whenever a class manages dynamic memory it usually needs

1. Destructor
2. Copy Constructor
3. Copy Assignment Operator

These three functions work together.

If you implement one,

you will almost always need the other two.

